=== tool delegate
Run a task in a sub-thread working in this folder. Use action=start to create one, or action=send with thread to continue one. Use it only when the person asks you to delegate, to use sub-agents, or to work on several things at once; never on your own initiative, and not for a new thread the person asks for.
- A new `general_purpose` sub-thread inherits your tools and mode. An `explorer` can only read with read, glob, grep and execute; its scripts have the same restriction. Neither kind delegates or reaches other threads.
- The sub-thread does not see this conversation: put everything it needs in `task`.
- The call waits and returns the sub-thread's final answer; several calls in one reply run at once. With `background`, it returns at once and you get a message when the sub-thread finishes.
- Every result names the sub-thread; to give it more work, use action=send with its id in `thread`. It keeps its original kind; you cannot change it by continuing.
- Its answer is not proof: check what it changed before you report the work done.
- The person sees every sub-thread and can steer it.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "SubThreadKind": {
      "description": "A sub-thread's fixed scope, chosen when it is created and retained for every later task.",
      "enum": [
        "explorer",
        "general_purpose"
      ],
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Start an independent sub-thread with a fixed kind.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "start",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "background": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Return at once and report completion to the parent instead of waiting.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "kind": {
          "$ref": "#/$defs/SubThreadKind",
          "default": "general_purpose",
          "description": "Explorer is read-only; `general_purpose` inherits the parent's tools and mode."
        },
        "task": {
          "description": "Everything the new sub-thread needs; it does not see this conversation.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "title": {
          "description": "A short title shown to the person.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "task"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Give one of your existing sub-threads more work; its kind cannot change.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "send",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "background": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Return at once and report completion to the parent instead of waiting.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "task": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "thread": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "thread",
        "task"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool edit
Replace text in a file. `old` must match exactly one place, or every place with `all`; include just enough lines to make it unique. Matching tries the exact text, then with typographic characters (curly quotes, dashes, special spaces) read as plain ones, then line by line ignoring trailing whitespace; an inexact match is noted. Returns the change as a unified diff. If `old` is not found, the closest lines in the file are shown, or a note says `new` is already there.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "all": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Replace every match.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "new": {
      "description": "Replacement text.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "old": {
      "description": "Text to replace, exactly as read returned it.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "File: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path",
    "old",
    "new"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool execute
Run JavaScript as the body of an async function, with your tools on `tools`: `await tools.read({ path: "a.txt" })` returns { content, structuredContent?, metadata? } and rejects on failure. Use loops, Promise.all and code to work on the results. Text and images reach the model through explicit text(value), console.log, image(contentBlock), and the return value. No return or return undefined emits no value; explicit null remains null. Images use input_image blocks from tool results; metadata is not sent automatically. File delivery links are retained automatically, including when the script throws. Use it for work of many steps: reading or changing many files, many browser actions, batch extraction. `tools` has each of your tools by name, except execute and tools that ask the person or start threads, plus the tools listed in <catalog>.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "code": {
      "description": "JavaScript run as the body of an async function; its return value is the result.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "code"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool git
Inspect the workspace repository and manage linked worktrees. status returns the repository and uncommitted changes; list_worktrees lists registered paths, branches and locks. create_worktree creates a new directory and branch from a committed base (HEAD by default); uncommitted work stays in its original directory. remove_worktree removes only a clean, unlocked linked worktree and keeps its branch. Neither operation switches this thread's workspace or context. Use shell for commit, push, merge and other Git commands. Creation failures may leave a directory or branch: inspect before retrying; nothing is automatically deleted.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Repository, branch and uncommitted changes in the thread's workspace.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "status",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Registered worktrees, including lock and pruning information.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "list_worktrees",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Create a new linked worktree and branch from a committed revision. Does not switch the thread.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "create_worktree",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "base": {
          "description": "Commit, branch or tag to start from; HEAD if omitted.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "branch": {
          "description": "New literal branch name.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "path": {
          "description": "New directory; relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~/. Its parent must exist.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "path",
        "branch"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Remove a clean, unlocked linked worktree, retaining its branch. Never forces removal.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "remove_worktree",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "path": {
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "path"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool glob
Find files under a directory whose paths match a glob, respecting ignore files. Returns one path per line, newest first, 200 per page; a note gives the offset of the next page.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "offset": {
      "description": "First result to return, from 1, newest first.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "Directory to search: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder. Defaults to\nthe workspace.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "Glob pattern, such as src/**/*.rs.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool grep
Search file contents with a regular expression (ripgrep syntax), in the files under a directory or in one file, respecting ignore files. Returns matches as `path:line: text` and context lines as `path:line- text`, 250 matches per page; a note gives the offset of the next page. Lines over 500 characters are truncated. Binary files are skipped and counted.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "case_insensitive": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Ignore case.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "context": {
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Lines of context to show around each match.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 0,
      "type": "integer"
    },
    "glob": {
      "description": "Search only files matching this glob; a glob without / matches file names.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "literal": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Treat `pattern` as plain text.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "First match to return, from 1, files ordered by path.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "default": "",
      "description": "Directory or file to search: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.\nDefaults to the workspace.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "pattern": {
      "description": "Regular expression in ripgrep syntax, or plain text with `literal`.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "pattern"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool present_file
Present one file in this conversation. Use after generating a report, image, archive, or other result. Accepts a readable regular file up to 32 MiB; directories are refused. The saved file survives changes to the source. Non-HTML files keep their original bytes. HTML embeds referenced local images into the saved copy; the source file is unchanged. This does not publish the file on the internet.

HTML appears inline above the final reply. Do not announce or restate the page.

Layout:
- The frame is the reply's width, including on phones. Use fluid widths. Leave html, body, and the outer element without a background color, outer card, border, banner title, or side padding. The viewer provides the background and an overridable 8px gutter.
- Give boxes that need their own background 16px padding and var(--radius) corners. Charts may have fixed pixel heights.
- Let content determine height. Do not use viewport-height units or height:100% on html, body, or the outer page.

Theme:
- Prefer theme CSS variables by default: --background, --foreground, --muted, --card, --popover, --secondary, --border, --input, --ring, --primary, --accent, --destructive, --warning, --success, --info, --code-background. Most have matching -foreground variables; accent, destructive, and warning also have -surface variants. Other colors, including fixed colors, are welcome when they suit the content or design; keep text readable against its background in light and dark themes.
- Use --chart-1 through --chart-6, --radius, --font-sans, and --font-mono. The viewer supplies the base background, text and fonts, and updates variables with the conversation theme.
- Inherit the base typography. For explicit sizes use --text-body (14px), --text-body--line-height (1.625), --text-heading-1 (16px), --text-heading-2 (15px), --text-heading-3 (14px), and --text-heading--line-height (1.3). Keep ordinary report metrics and counters at the h1 size; emphasize with weight and color. Use larger typography only when requested for a poster or website design.
- Give var() sensible fallback values for opening the downloaded file outside Octoii. Do not redefine the theme variables themselves.

Resources and checking:
- Prefer inline styles and scripts. HTTP/HTTPS images, fonts, styles, scripts and fetch requests are allowed; remote URLs load as-is and require connectivity. Use quoted local image paths (relative to the HTML file or absolute) or CSS url(...); readable image files are embedded before preview and delivery. Use one src per local image, not srcset. Local stylesheets and scripts are not bundled: inline them or use remote URLs.
- Use browser.preview before presenting when available. Check desktop and phone widths, console messages and interactive controls. Check both light and dark appearance when possible. Fix clipping or horizontal overflow, then present the finished file once.

--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "path": {
      "description": "Regular file to present: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "minLength": 1,
      "type": "string"
    },
    "title": {
      "description": "Optional display title. The downloadable file keeps its original basename.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool question
Ask the person one to eight questions at once. A question can offer options to choose one of, or several with `multiple`, or no options for a free-text answer. The person may answer in their own words, skip questions, or dismiss them all. Put your recommended option first and add "(recommended)" to its label. Ask only what you cannot find out yourself and what changes what you do.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Asked": {
      "description": "A question.",
      "properties": {
        "header": {
          "description": "A label of one or two words, shown above the question.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "multiple": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Allow choosing several options.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "options": {
          "description": "Options to choose from; omit for a free-text answer.",
          "items": {
            "$ref": "#/$defs/Choice"
          },
          "type": "array"
        },
        "question": {
          "description": "The question, as the person will read it.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "question"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "Choice": {
      "description": "An option.",
      "properties": {
        "description": {
          "description": "What the option means, where the label is not enough.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
        "label": {
          "description": "The option, in a few words.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "label"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "questions": {
      "description": "One to eight questions.",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/Asked"
      },
      "maxItems": 8,
      "minItems": 1,
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "questions"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool read
Read a file, or list a directory (directory names end in /). Text is returned as is, without line numbers, up to 2000 lines or 48 KB per call; lines over 2000 characters are truncated. A note after the text gives the lines returned and the offset to continue from. Images are shown to you; PDFs and other binary files are named, not shown.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "limit": {
      "description": "Number of lines or entries to return.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "offset": {
      "description": "First line or entry to return, from 1.",
      "format": "uint",
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "path": {
      "description": "File or directory: relative to the workspace, absolute, or starting with ~ for the home folder.",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "path"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool shell
Run a bash command in the workspace (no need to cd there). Its input is closed. Returns stdout and stderr together, then a note of how it ended: exit N, a signal, or a timeout. The time limit is 120 seconds; set `timeout_seconds` for up to 600. For reading, searching and editing files, use read, grep, glob and edit instead. In default mode commands run in a sandbox that can write only the workspace and the folders <environment> lists; to write anywhere else (such as a global install), run the command again with `escalate` and a `justification`, and the person decides.
--- parameters
{
  "properties": {
    "command": {
      "description": "The command to run.",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "escalate": {
      "default": false,
      "description": "Run outside the sandbox, once the person approves.",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    "justification": {
      "description": "Why it must write outside the sandbox, in one sentence for the person.",
      "type": [
        "string",
        "null"
      ]
    },
    "timeout_seconds": {
      "description": "Time limit in seconds; 120 by default, at most 600.",
      "format": "uint64",
      "maximum": 600,
      "minimum": 1,
      "type": [
        "integer",
        "null"
      ]
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "command"
  ],
  "type": "object"
}

=== tool terminal
Work the thread's terminals, which the person sees beside the chat.
- open: run a command that does not exit by itself (a server, a watcher) in a new terminal in the workspace, sandboxed as shell commands are. Returns the terminal's number and its screen after a moment. When the command exits you get a message with how it ended and its last screen; do not poll for it.
- read: return what a terminal shows, the person's terminals included.
- write: type into a terminal you opened (`\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\r` for Enter); returns the screen after.
- close: close a terminal you opened, ending its command.
For a command that exits by itself, use shell. Link a terminal in your reply as :terminal{id=<number>}, or as ::terminal{id=<number>} on its own line to show its card with its last lines.
--- parameters
{
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "Run a command in a new terminal.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "open",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "command": {
          "description": "The command to run.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "escalate": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Run outside the sandbox, once the person approves.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "justification": {
          "description": "Why it must write outside the sandbox, in one sentence for the person.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "command"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Return what a terminal shows, the person's terminals included.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "read",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Type into a terminal you opened.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "write",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "text": {
          "description": "Keys as text: `\\u0003` for Ctrl-C, `\\r` for Enter.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal",
        "text"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Close a terminal you opened, ending its command.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "close",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "terminal": {
          "description": "The terminal's number.",
          "format": "uint32",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "terminal"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  ]
}

=== tool thread
Work with the person's other threads, which they see in their thread list.
- list: list the threads, the most recently active first: each one's id, how it stands, when it was last active, its project and its title.
- search: find what was said in the threads: the words the person, the threads and the models said and the calls the models made that hold `keywords` — all of them, or any with `matching: any` — the newest first, each with its thread, its id and the words around the match; `thread` searches one thread alone, and this thread is searched only when named so.
- read: read a thread's conversation — what the person and the model said, each call and how it ended — the newest page first; `around` an entry's id from search reads the conversation about it; page with `before` or `after` as a page's note says.
- start: create a thread in this folder with your model and mode; `message` is its first input.
- send: send a thread a message; it takes it at its next step, or starts a turn with it.
- stop: stop the turn a thread is working on.
- rename: request a new, nonempty title; it applies when the thread takes its next step.
- pin/unpin, archive/unarchive, snooze/unsnooze: organize the person's thread list without starting or stopping work. Snooze requires a future ISO timestamp with an explicit UTC offset. These actions and rename can target this thread too.
Use it only when the person asks about threads, asks for a new thread, or asks to message, stop or organize a named thread; never for sub-agents, delegation or parallel work. To find what was said about something earlier, search for it rather than page through a thread. A new thread does not see this conversation: put everything it needs in `message`. With `report`, you get a message when the turn that takes your message ends; do not wait for it or poll. Link a thread in your reply as :thread{id=<id>}, or as ::thread{id=<id>} on its own line to show its card.
--- parameters
{
  "$defs": {
    "Matching": {
      "description": "How a text holds keywords: every one of them — AND — or any one — OR.",
      "enum": [
        "all",
        "any"
      ],
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "oneOf": [
    {
      "description": "List the person's threads, the most recently active first.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "list",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "archived": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "List archived threads too.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "offset": {
          "default": 0,
          "description": "Threads to skip, for the next page.",
          "format": "uint",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Find what was said and each call made in the threads that holds keywords, the newest first.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "search",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "archived": {
          "default": false,
          "description": "Search archived threads too.",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "keywords": {
          "description": "The words to find, separated by spaces, whatever their case.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "matching": {
          "$ref": "#/$defs/Matching",
          "default": "all",
          "description": "Whether an entry holds every keyword (`all`, AND) or any one (`any`, OR)."
        },
        "offset": {
          "default": 0,
          "description": "Entries to skip, for the next page.",
          "format": "uint",
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer"
        },
        "thread": {
          "description": "Search this thread's id alone.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "keywords"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    {
      "description": "Read a thread's conversation: what was said and each call made, a page at a time, the newest page first.",
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "const": "read",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "after": {
          "description": "Read the page after this entry's id, as a page's note gives it.",
          "type": [
            "string",
            "null"
          ]
        },
